POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V4.1 for Digital UNIX

HIGHLIGHTS

o  NetView V4.1 uses a client/server model to distribute network management
   applications

o  Enhanced security in the distributed environment is provided via DCE
   security services

o  A distributed architecture enables significant customization of the user
   interface, including maintaining local maps on client systems

o  New event correlation capability simplifies problem diagnosis by allowing
   the network manager to recognize the relationship between multiple events

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V4.1 for Digital UNIX software (SPD 53.57)
uses a client/server model to provide a distributed network management
solution for heterogeneous multivendor devices and open networks requiring
Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP). It includes user oriented SNMP
applications for fault, configuration, and performance management; and
standard application programming interfaces that allow software developers
to seamlessly integrate their multiprotocol applications.

V4.1 provides support for a range of databases including INGRES, Oracle,
SYBASE, INFORMIX, and DB/2 databases.

V4.1 enhancements

o  Client/server capabilities -- Through client/server capabilities, V4.1
   provides the flexibility to centralize or decentralize the network
   management function as appropriate for the organization. In this
   client/server model, the server performs various "centralized" functions,
   such as network discovery, historical data collection, and status
   polling. The client offloads the server of user-oriented processing; for
   example, the client directly issues SNMP requests during MIB (Management
   Information Base) browsing. With the new client/server capabilities,
   operators can share common applications and data residing on servers, but
   also have the option of keeping local maps on client machines to
   customize their view of the network.

o  Distributed security -- In a distributed client/server application
   environment, it is critical to implement a security architecture that
   protects against unauthorized user access to NetView software and
   establishes appropriate privileges for the operators. V4.1 uses DCE
   security services to provide secure access to network management
   services. DCE is based on Kerberos technology and uses encryption to
   ensure that sensitive security information is never passed in clear text
   over vulnerable media. DCE provides the capability to set up user groups
   that are allowed access to only a specified set of applications and
   operations. Therefore, logging in via DCE provides users with customized
   tool palettes, menu bars, and context menus. DCE security mechanisms can
   be invoked via the industry standard GSS-API, which provides a uniform
   interface for independent software vendors developing applications with
   built-in security. V4.1 also provides an audit trail of various key
   events, such as login attempts, configuration changes, and access to key
   functions and applications.

o  Event correlation -- Event correlation is a powerful new capability that
   allows the network manager to take action based on information from
   multiple, related events. Network events often trigger related events;
   for example, several "adjacency down" events can be triggered when a node
   crashes. Using the event correlation editor to set up filters that relate
   several discrete events makes it easier to determine the true problem.
   Another example is that certain events may not need operator intervention
   unless they occur repeatedly. With event correlation, users can set up
   filters that display events with an appropriate level of severity if they
   have occurred repeatedly over a period of time.

o  User interface -- In addition to the customization of the user interface,
   which allows for customized menus and tool palettes, other key
   enhancements have been made to the user interface. These include the
   color coding of event cards; improved zooming ability, which allows the
   user to zoom in on a group of objects; selective hiding of objects; easy
   accessibility to object descriptions; and color support in the print
   tool.

o  Object collection facility -- The object collection facility allows the
   operator to define groups of objects; for example, all wide area routers
   could be defined as a single group. Groups can be defined based on
   network addresses or other object attributes, such as device type. The
   object collection facility, therefore, streamlines operator queries by
   allowing operations to be performed on a group as a whole.

o  SNMP V2.0 -- SNMP V2.0 is a new Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF)
   standard that is in the process of being finalized. It offers several
   advantages over V1.0, including superior performance by transferring more
   information with a single request; manager to manager communication by
   providing a communications standard between SNMP management applications;
   and greatly enhanced security to protect against unauthorized management
   actions. SNMP V2.0 has been anticipated for some time; as this standard
   is completed, it will be implemented in NetView software. SNMP V2.0 will
   be implemented in an update to V4.1, which is expected to be available to
   V4.1 users later this year. The update will implement the new WinSnmp API
   and the SNMP V2.0 protocol stack.

o  Backup manager -- The backup manager has been enhanced to provide
   automatic discovery and mapping of other NetView servers, and includes
   additional capability for managing specific domains and closing them upon
   return of the remote manager.

Complementary solutions

POLYCENTER DECnet Manager V2.1 for Digital UNIX software (SPD 62.53) is
available as a layered product on POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V4.1. The
POLYCENTER DECnet Manager product consists of network management
applications for managing DECnet Phase IV using Network Control Program
(NCP) software; DECnet/OSI using Network Command Language (NCL); Digital's
LAN bridges using Remote Bridge Management Software (RBMS); and Digital's
terminal servers using Console Carrier Request (CCR) software.

POLYCENTER Pathdoctor (SPD 51.29) software is an interactive SNMP based
network management tool. It assists users in evaluating the health and
performance of Internet Protocol (IP) routing paths between specified TCP/IP
address pairs. Pathdoctor software is particularly adept at determining
routing problems and inefficiencies for Cisco, Wellfleet, 3Com, Proteon,
Digital, and IBM routers.

Migration packages enabling users to move from DECmcc (POLYCENTER Network
Manager) software to POLYCENTER Manager on NetView software continue to be
offered.

BENEFITS

Digital's SNMP network users will have more flexibility and more powerful
network management capabilities when using V4.1 of POLYCENTER Manager on
NetView for Digital UNIX software. A considerable number of product
enhancements have been developed and implemented jointly by Digital and IBM
in this distributed version of NetView software. The new distributed
architecture will lead to significant benefits, including improved
performance through the off-loading of client-oriented processing functions
from the server; an increased number of operators can work off of the same
network server; and they can customize the user interface to fit their
roles.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V4.1 will begin shipping on September 1, 1995.
Digital UNIX V3.2 or V3.2B is a prerequisite. Digital provides a warranty
for this software with the purchase of a license.

